This paper investigates the generalized Bi-Choquard equation on $\mathbb{R}^{N}$, given by \begin{align*}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^{2} u - \Delta u = \frac{1}{\epsilon^{2(N-\theta))} \left( \int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}} \frac{(1+|x|^{2})^{-\theta} |u|^{2} dx}{|x|^{N-2\alpha}} \right) u |u|^{2N-4}, \quad u \in C^{4}(\mathbb{R}^{N}), 
\end{array}
\right. \end{align*} under certain conditions and the parameter $\epsilon$ small enough. We leverage a new approach to establish a standardized ground state solution via a construction method, given the equation's critical nature and the Bi-Choquard equation's complex properties. Additionally, we demonstrate that this standardized ground state solution meets a specific symmetry condition when the parameter $\epsilon$ approaches zero.

